  "studyTitle": "Action Recognition Using Vision Transformer with Compressible Video Encryption",
  "studySubtitle": "Proposal of an Encryption-then-Compression (EtC) System Compatible with H.264/AVC Video Compression and Vision Transformer",
  "studyFig1Caption": "Fig. 1: Overall Architecture of the Proposed Video EtC System",
  "studyBody1": "With the rapid expansion of cloud computing and machine learning, computer vision and action recognition services deployed on cloud servers have become increasingly widespread. However, transmitting video data to cloud servers exposes sensitive visual information to severe privacy risks, including potential interception during transmission, data leakage from cloud storage, and reconstruction attacks attempting to recover training data or personal identities from model outputs.\n\nBecause video data has an enormous file size compared to text or audio, video compression using standards such as H.264/AVC is indispensable to reduce storage costs, save network bandwidth, and minimize transmission latency. However, standard encryption algorithms like AES and RSA cannot perform compression after encryption (Encryption-then-Compression: EtC) because lossy compression artifacts propagate catastrophically across entire ciphertexts due to strong diffusion properties.\n\nTo overcome this limitation, this research extends block-based EtC techniques—originally proposed for still images—to video, establishing a novel video EtC system tailored for H.264/AVC compression and Vision Transformer (ViT) action recognition. The encryption keys are generated and managed strictly on the client side. By transmitting encrypted, H.264-compressed video alongside an encrypted ViT model, cloud providers can execute highly accurate action recognition in the encrypted domain without ever accessing decryption keys or viewing plaintext videos.",
  "studyFig2Caption": "Fig. 2: Flow of Proposed Video EtC Encryption Process",
  "studyBody2": "Conventional video encryption methods (e.g., LCVE) randomize pixels directly, destroying spatial and temporal correlations and causing severe degradation in video compression efficiency. The proposed method designs a multi-stage cube-based encryption scheme carefully coordinated with H.264 predictive coding and ViT tubelet input structures.\n\nSpecifically, the video is first partitioned into non-overlapping 3D hexahedra (cubes of size 16×16×2) matching the H.264 macroblock size (16×16) and the ViT tubelet temporal dimension (t=2), followed by sub-cube subdivision (8×8×2). Geometric transformations (cube scrambling, rotation, and inversion) are restricted to cube units to preserve local intra-frame correlation, avoiding failure in H.264 spatial prediction. Meanwhile, perceptual confidentiality is achieved through sub-cube pixel negative-positive inversion and RGB channel permutation, retaining channel-wise statistical properties to minimize compression overhead.\n\nFurthermore, to prevent inter-frame prediction failures across scrambled cube boundaries, the Group of Pictures (GoP) is optimized by placing I-frames at even intervals (GoP=2) synchronized with the cube temporal length. In experimental evaluations using VideoMAEv2-Base, the proposed method achieved a Top-1 classification accuracy of 87.66%, completely matching the accuracy on plaintext video (87.66%) with zero degradation. Moreover, under H.264 compression, the system maintained superior classification accuracy even at practical low bitrates (approx. 0.5 bpp), successfully demonstrating the coexistence of privacy preservation, high compression efficiency, and high-accuracy AI recognition.",
